## The standing criticism of the last attempt

The conjecture misread the rule. It treated "object" as any JSON value, so it counted the fenced list [1, 2] as the last fenced object. The rule's word "object" means a JSON object (a dict); a list is not an object. The code's recover_json_object only returns dict values and skips non-dict candidates, so it returns the last fenced dict {"a": 1}. The execution confirms this: the input with two fenced blocks returned ({'a': 1}, ()) unchanged. Thus the rule as written and the code do not part company here; the conjecture's expected move was based on the misreading. A next conjecture should avoid repeating the list-as-object reading. Instead, test the boundary the rule actually draws: a fenced JSON object after a fenced non-object, or a fenced non-object after a fenced object, and confirm the last fenced dict is chosen. Quote: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text."

